{
  "requirement": "Each bundled skill's workflow MUST end by naming the bundled skill for the next task, or, when different outcomes lead to different tasks, the skill for each such outcome. An outcome that leads to no further task needs no skill named. Each bundled skill, recognized by its front matter, is a site:\n\n:::{sites} skills\n```yaml\nlanguage: markdown\npaths: [assets/skills]\nrule:\n  kind: minus_metadata\nmatch:\n  - \"---\\nname: example\\n---\\n\\n# Example\\n\"\nno_match:\n  - \"# Example\\n\"\n```\n:::",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement is distributed. Its obligation is that each bundled skill's workflow ends by naming the skill for the next task, or for each outcome that leads to a further task. Each skill upholds that obligation on its own, and each site has its own review. I found no obligation that neither a site nor particular code carries out.\n\nSite query `skills`: it uses `kind: minus_metadata` over `assets/skills` in Markdown.\n- It returns exactly five sites, the root of each `SKILL.md`: `habeas`, `habeas-requirements`, `habeas-evidence`, `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` and `habeas-refactor`.\n- I searched `assets` for files that start with `---\\nname:` front matter and for lines beginning `name: `. Both searches found the same five files and nothing else.\n- The `references/*.md` files carry no skill front matter, so they are correctly not selected. The query therefore neither misses a skill nor selects anything out of scope.\n\nQuery snippets: the match snippet is a document with `name` front matter and a heading, which the query selects. The no_match snippet is the same body without front matter, which it rejects. Together they show the front-matter condition that decides what counts as a site. No violation queries are declared.\n\nThe extra test is `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bundled_skill_workflows_end_by_naming_the_skill_for_the_next_task]`, marked `{test}`. It asserts that each of the five skills' last workflow step contains the named next skill: habeas-evidence, habeas-requirements, or \"Follow that skill\" for the routing table. It also asserts that the habeas routing table names every other skill. This adds checking beyond the per-site reviews and I found no gap in it.",
  "references": "cad77bef8fd9a6e827e81314c255639bcbcc74e3c865da250136997ac253fa61",
  "inputs": [
    {
      "requirements": "requirements.cli.skill_conventions.next",
      "matches": [
        "requirements.cli.skill_conventions.next"
      ],
      "text": [
        "::::{req} skill_conventions.next",
        "Each bundled skill's workflow MUST end by naming the bundled skill for the next task, or, when different outcomes lead to different tasks, the skill for each such outcome. An outcome that leads to no further task needs no skill named. Each bundled skill, recognized by its front matter, is a site:",
        "",
        ":::{sites} skills",
        "```yaml",
        "language: markdown",
        "paths: [assets/skills]",
        "rule:",
        "  kind: minus_metadata",
        "match:",
        "  - \"---\\nname: example\\n---\\n\\n# Example\\n\"",
        "no_match:",
        "  - \"# Example\\n\"",
        "```",
        ":::",
        "::::"
      ]
    },
